    "created_at": "2022-05-02T00:10:09Z",
    "updated_at": "2022-05-02T00:10:09Z",
    "body": "This one seems challenging. I'm not familiar with the mechanics of Samba, but with NFS (which can suffer similar-looking problems during temporary network outages), you have the problem that you have to know that it's an NFS-mounted file before attempting to access it. Once you initiate the access, you're at the mercy of the system as to when (or even if) the call will time out.\r\n\r\nIt seems to me that the best we could do in the short term is to implement something like #1891, which would allow a user to completely exclude a Samba path from being scanned by starship, at the cost of not allowing starship to run there even when it's online.\r\n\r\nOtherwise, it seems we'd need some combination of the following three abilities:\r\n\r\n1. The ability to tell whether a directory tree exists as a remote share or not.\r\n2. A way to *non-blocking-ly* tell whether the remote share is currently available.\r\n3. Some way to selectively override the mechanism.\r\n\r\nAm I overthinking this? Is there some lovely quick hack we could make to solve this issue?",
    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
    "pin": null,
